            "type": "p",
            "html": "<span class=\"kt\">Dead reckoning</span> filled in the remaining gaps. By tracking a ship's known speed (estimated by timing how long it took a floating object thrown from the bow to reach the stern), compass bearing, and time elapsed, a navigator could calculate an estimated current position. Over short distances dead reckoning was reasonably accurate; over long ocean voyages, errors accumulated. But combined with compass bearings and periodic latitude fixes from the astrolabe, dead reckoning gave navigators a working, if imprecise, sense of position in open water."
